    - io_boundary  # e47d
    isbefore: []  # e47d
    duplicate_of: []  # e47d
    not_duplicate_of: []  # e47d
    pr_ref:  # e47d
    description: "Found by WI-baran's arm-A census (2026-09-06, ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/baran_sockets_09062026/summarize_A.py). Phase 6 recall, INV-linub's class in C: a catalogued call site that cannot reach its row for want of module evidence.\n\nTHE GAP. c.yaml lists `send` and `recv` under ambiguous_names (\"socket send vs project-local send()\"), and the C analyzer fills no module slot from #include, so every bare call is `c:external:0-0:send:unresolved` and gate_named_entry refuses it (io_boundary.py: `if ambiguous_names and name in ambiguous_names: return None`). On the twelve-repo corpus 156 of 324 send*/recv* sites classify as NOTHING in either arm: dbus-broker 15/17, haproxy 28/48, passt 23/43, torsocks 3/7, libvfio-user 4/6, guacamole-server 4/7, firejail 7/23. sendto/sendmsg/recvfrom/recvmsg reach the catalogue; send/recv do not. The WI-baran stamp lands on them (16 `pipe` stamps on bare send/recv in arm B) and moves nothing, because there is no classification to move.\n\nTHE GUARD IS RIGHT AS A GUARD: a project-local `send()` exists (fluent-bit's vendored nuttx shim is the live example, see the sibling item), and name-only matching would credit it. What is missing is the EVIDENCE that lifts the ambiguity: the file `#include <sys/socket.h>` and the repository defines no function named send. That is receiver/module evidence, the same shape as INV-misup's `new`-constructed receiver and WI-nasuf's declared types -- resolvable at the analyzer, not at the catalogue.\n\nTHE FIX SHAPE, not designed here: c.py records the file's system includes and, for a call to a name that is in ambiguous_names, stamps the module slot (`c:sys/socket:0-0:send:unresolved`) when <sys/socket.h> is included and the resolver finds no in-repo definition of that name. Then the existing qualified-name path classifies it and the ambiguity guard is never reached. Same for unistd read/write/close (also in ambiguous_names) -- check the analogues before shipping (LIVE rule 4).\n\nMEASURED THROUGH THE TAINT PATH (INV-linub 2026-09-04): this ADDS sources (recv is net_recv/ipc_recv) and sinks; marginal precision on the new findings, awaits_bakeoff_validation on the PR."  # e47d
    fields: {}  # e47d
- op: discuss  # ad7f
  at: '2026-09-09T18:06:31Z'  # ad7f
  by: agent  # ad7f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ad7f
  clock: 2  # ad7f
  nonce: ad7f  # ad7f
  message: "DONE 2026-09-09, merged at dev c9a566a9bd (fix 8e22832b31, measurement 0023).\nTagged awaits_bakeoff_validation: this carries a quantitative improvement claim.\n\nTHE FIX WAS PARITY, NOT THE NEW DESIGN THIS ITEM SKETCHED. The item proposed\nc.py record system includes and stamp the module slot. That mechanism ALREADY\nEXISTED and C was the only language not using it: cpp.py has stamped the\ncomma-joined #include set since WI-rupik / WI-mafik, and _module_hint_candidates\n(INV-funuf) already splits the slot on commas AND normalises sys/socket.h to the\nsys/socket spelling c.yaml declares. The consumer side needed no change at all.\nC -- whose catalogue cpp INHERITS -- simply never stamped.\n\nTHE IN-REPO GUARD NEEDED NO NEW CODE EITHER. The item asked for \"the resolver\nfinds no in-repo definition of that name\"; the stamp sits on the else branch of\nresolver.lookup(...).found and the resolver is built over repo-wide\nglobal_symbols, so a project defining its own send resolves to it and never\nreaches the stamp. The fluent-bit nuttx shim case is closed by construction and\nis pinned by a test.\n\nMEASURED, both arms one FILE apart with the c.py blob asserted at BEGIN and END\n(arm exits 3 on mismatch), full record in\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/lajus_include_09092026/ and docs/measurements/0023:\n\n    passt          io_edges  75 -> 119   NEW sys/socket.{send,recv,connect,\n                                             accept}, unistd.{read,write,fork,stat}\n    libvfio-user   io_edges  51 ->  58   NEW sys/socket.{recv,connect,accept},\n                                             sys/stat.stat\n    torsocks       io_edges  15 ->  16   NEW sys/stat.stat\n\n13 new primitives, ZERO lost. send and recv -- this item's filed population --\nreach for the first time: 7 and 7 on passt, 4 on libvfio-user.\n\nTHE COST, REPORTED AS A FIRST-CLASS RESULT: 15 of 21 claim verdicts moved\nconfirmed_with_caveats -> inconclusive; ZERO moved toward confirmed. Findings\nare unchanged in every repo (50/6/11), so there is NO precision number here and\nnone is claimed -- the item asked for marginal precision on new findings and\nthere are no new findings, only newly classified boundaries.\n\nI NEARLY PUBLISHED THE FLATTERING EXPLANATION. \"The change added sources, so\nvacuously confirmed claims became live\" is WRONG, and torsocks refutes it: no\nnet_recv/ipc_recv chains in EITHER arm, six claims moved anyway, and\ncommand_launch is unchanged in all three repos (77/9/2). The mechanism is\nINV-zimud, already documented in verify_claims.py: the coverage gate expands a\ndisjunctive module slot with ALL, not ANY, and its own comment records the\npopulation it was measured on -- \"the three C repos 0.0%\". C dsts carried no\ncommas so it never bit C; this change makes every ambiguous call carry one, and\nc.yaml declares no module_completeness for any header to vouch for them.\n\nA REGRESSION THIS MEASUREMENT CAUGHT BEFORE IT SHIPPED. The first\nimplementation stamped EVERY unresolved call. A module hint SUPPRESSES the\npermissive short-name fallback and C reaches most of libc through QUOTED project\nheaders, so a slot naming only the system includes made the module filter refuse\nrows that used to match: on qemu-dtc stdio.fclose 5 -> 2, stdio.fopen 1 -> 0,\nstdio.fprintf 35 -> 34, stdio.fputc 6 -> 5. PARTIAL EVIDENCE IS WORSE THAN NONE.\nThis item's own text specified restricting the stamp to ambiguous_names; the\nitem was right and I over-generalised. Both halves are pinned by tests.\n\nRESIDUALS FILED, NOT FOLDED IN:\n  WI-hasul  c.yaml declares NO module_completeness at all, so no C module is\n            ever an examined negative. This is what makes the verdict labels\n            move, and closing it returns them without loosening any gate.\n  WI-bulub  open and close are in ambiguous_names with NO catalogue row, so\n            they can never classify at any quality of module evidence -- 11\n            sites on qemu-dtc alone. L1, not L3/L4.\n\nNOT MEASURED, stated because it is this item's own largest filed population:\nhaproxy (28 of 48 sites, 30 MB) was not run, and dbus-broker /\nguacamole-server / firejail are not in this corpus. The recall direction is\nstructural and unlikely to reverse; the 15-of-21 verdict movement rate is a\nproperty of today's empty c.yaml completeness block, not of this change, and\nshould be re-read after WI-hasul."  # ad7f
